From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abhay-byte <zenithblue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 06:48:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] panvk: speed up the BC lowmem transcoder and add
 PANVK_DEBUG=bc_timing

The ETC2/EAC transcoder of csf-v11/197 searched the ETC2/EAC code space
per texel (16 decoded texels x every table, modifier and base); on the
G615 that put BC1-BC5 uploads at 16-43 ms per 1024x1024 chain against
1.4-4.1 ms for the RC4-lowmem decode path. It is now a palette encoder:
BC1-BC5 blocks have at most 4 colours / 8 alpha values, so every search
runs on the palette and the texels are remapped at the end.

 - EAC (BC2/BC3 alpha, BC4, BC5): the base codeword is fitted to the 8
   palette values (centre, then least squares on the chosen modifiers),
   the (table, multiplier) candidates come from a LUT indexed by the
   palette shape (EAC_TM_LUT, trained offline by tests/bc-host/gen_eac_lut
   over all BC4 endpoint pairs), the nearest modifier per value from a
   second LUT (EAC_NEAR); the error is exact (clamped, palette counts).
   PANVK_BC_LM_OPTS bits 20-21 pick 1-3 candidates (default 1).
 - ETC2 RGB (BC1-BC3): the 4 BC1 colours are kept unpacked; the flip is
   chosen from the half-block sums (both with 0x40000), ETC1 tables by a
   1D luma search over the 3 tables around the colour spread (all 8 with
   0x80000), H mode seeded from the BC1 endpoints with 3 distances around
   their spread, planar from the palette moments; every candidate error
   is exact over the palette with per-colour counts.
 - one pipeline per transcode mode: the mode and the options are
   specialization constants (2, 3) instead of push constants, so each
   pipeline only holds its own path; loops are unrolled ([[unroll]]), the
   per-block arrays are scalars (no dynamically indexed locals), loads and
   stores are uvec2/uvec4, and block addresses are 64-bit (z * slice no
   longer overflows 32 bits).

Quality against 200 (tests/bc-host/bench_etc2, PSNR of the ETC2/EAC
decode against the exact BC decode, 65536 blocks per set, default opts
0x30000), and host time per block:

  set      BC1    BC1A   BC2a   BC3a   BC4U   BC4S   (dB delta)
  random  -0.43  -0.11  -0.16  -0.09  -2.33  -2.37
  pic0    -0.15  -0.14  -1.02  -0.17  -1.10  -3.18
  pic2    -0.70  -0.70  -0.74  -0.64  +1.95  -4.14
  pic3    -0.08  -0.08  -1.87  -0.08  +2.12  -3.52
  host time: BC1 2.6-7.3x, BC2/BC3 3.4-8.8x, BC4/BC5 5.6-8.4x faster,
  5.8x overall

With 3 EAC candidates (PANVK_BC_LM_OPTS=0x230000) EAC is within
-0.45..+10.1 dB of 200 (BC4S pic3 -1.58) at 2x the EAC cost. SPIR-V ops
per block after specialization and optimization (default opts): BC1
2311, BC1A 2284, BC2 3130, BC3 3006, BC4 843, BC4S 835, BC5 1573, BC5S
1557 (BC4 1792 with 3 candidates).

The workgroup size is a specialization constant too, PANVK_BC_LM_WG=XxY
(default 8x8, x * y <= 256), part of the pipeline key, for device tuning.

PANVK_DEBUG=bc_timing: two ALL_COMMANDS timestamps (device-wide query
pool of 4096 slots, created lazily) around every BC upload / BC image
copy, read back when the command buffer is reset or freed (it can not be
pending then) and logged as
  panvk: bc_timing <format> <w>x<h> regions=N layers=N xcode|decode: X us
plus a running total every 64 operations. It serializes the queue around
each operation and is meant for measurements only.

Teardown goes through panvk_per_arch(device_bc_finish)(), which replaces
the panvk_meta_cleanup() call of destroy_device on the same line, so the
jm-v9 hunks in panvk_vX_device.c keep their line numbers.

Tested GPU: none (host tests: tests/bc-host test_etc2 0 failures,
bitstream and exact-error model checked against the Mesa ETC2/EAC
decoder for every mode; bench_etc2 table above)
Mesa base range: 5a07217f034b3e50d8c7c7794f97a2df1742613b
Dependencies: csf-v11/197
Validation test: tests/bc-host (make run); device: PanProbe bc_decode
 (ETC2/EAC shadow within the PSNR gate) and bc_perf with
 PANVK_DEBUG=bc_timing (upload ms per format)

Generated-by: LLM
